sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: stage 2 of the command development pipeline. A task VM, where an agent or Nick develops a command and the proof cases run too, holds a nested fleet (a Gateway with Swamp, an app-dev Node, an app-prod Node) that resets from the ready copies in the host's topology seed. The nested Nodes are Incus system containers with the outer VM's checkout as a disk device, so an edit reaches them at once with no sync; `base` is a container snapshot without memory (decided 2026-11-06). Uses the simple slot pool (task-vm.slots) as the target way to get the VM.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: development pipeline stage 2 · development environment
  %% kind: pipeline.2-development-environment
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  %% lane NT icon=server pill=fleet tone=gray
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>provision run
  participant H as Task VM host<br/>Incus over ssh
  participant VM as Task VM<br/>outer · operator
  participant NT as Nested fleet<br/>3 containers
  Note over GW: Admit the task<br/>group at a commit
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - an external task group in `todo` and a free task VM host
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the full intent is recorded; the group is reserved
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:
  %%> - a parked prep group is admitted by the gate check when the owner approves its spec: the Gateway appends the implementation subtask, moves the group to `running` and starts `tasks.build`, which resumes the VM, syncs the refs and runs the first implementer turn
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>tasks provision
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the same run for a person, an agent or a CI proof
  SW->>H: Take a task VM<br/>ready slot, else clone
  %%> Target, not built:
  %%> - start a ready slot that was enrolled ahead of time and kept stopped; takes seconds (chart `task-vm.slots`)
  %%> Built today:
  %%> - clone the task image, boot, enrol (keys, WireGuard, observer), model key, Pi; takes minutes
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:
  %%> - a task that needs the nested fleet (a proof, or any VM with test nodes: medium or large) is placed only on a host whose seed holds usable ready copies of the current format; with no such host free, admission answers `tasks.capacity_unavailable`
  %%> - the Gateway reads the fact from its own records: the seed follows the image the VM boots from, and it holds usable copies when the host's latest refresh run promoted that image and saved copies of the current `user.ready.format`; no agent report and no ssh
  %%> - the refresh of a host is pinned to that host and is not filtered, because it is what builds the copies; a task with no fleet (a plain task) still uses any host
  SW->>H: Attach the topology seed<br/>clone under the host lock
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - clones the seed of the host's task image; on ZFS the clone costs only what the Nodes write
  %%> - the VM sees it as the disk `topology-seed`
  %%> Why:
  %%> - each host keeps its own seed; nothing is copied between hosts
  SW->>VM: Check out the commit<br/>git fetch · task token
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks out the commit or branch the task works on, with a read token from the vault
  SW->>VM: Start the nested fleet<br/>from the ready copies
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - creates the nested network from the subnet stored with the ready copies
  %%> - per Node: copies the ready copy, adds its NIC with the stored MAC and address, attaches the outer VM's checkout as an Incus disk device (Gateway and app-dev), starts all three
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), container inner topology:
  %%> - the nested Nodes are Incus system containers, not VMs (chart `vm-fleet-build.mmd`); the outer task VM stays a VM
  %%> - no memory snapshot of the outer VM (Nick, option 3): a cold start of the outer VM plus the containers was as fast as its memory restore (beast 35 s, shark 27 s against 34 s), and a memory snapshot would need retaking per host and per release
  %%> - measured on shark (rvm-2): copies to a healthy fleet 8–11 s
  %%> - each ready copy is self-contained: its own `.env`, storage, `bootstrap/cache` and database on its own disk (stage 6 saves them so); the code comes from the outer VM's checkout
  %%> - needs no earlier fleet in this VM and no state from the outer VM; the task owns the network and the three containers
  %%> - then pushes the proof Swamp Club key into the nested Gateway, as today
  %%> Proposed 2026-11-05:
  %%> - replaces today's reset, which takes `.env`, storage, the agent manifest and the lease from an earlier fleet in the same VM, so it fails on a fresh VM
  %%> Time:
  %%> - about 10 seconds to a healthy fleet from the copies (8–11 s on shark); Doctor reads healthy once every agent has reported; an agent sends a report the Gateway did not accept again within seconds
  SW->>NT: Join as operator<br/>this VM's WireGuard key
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the outer VM makes its own WireGuard key; its public key goes into the nested Gateway's operator Node record with `incus exec` (the outer VM is the Incus host of the nested containers, so this needs no network yet)
  %%> - the nested Gateway puts the key on its hub; the outer VM brings up WireGuard as the operator `10.45.0.10`
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), container inner topology:
  %%> - the hub gets the operator peer through its peer file, which the nested Gateway renders, not through a runtime `wg set`: a restore of `base` reboots the containers, and a peer that lived only in memory was gone after it
  %%> - the private key never leaves this task VM; each task VM has its own
  %%> Proposed 2026-11-05:
  %%> - the ready copies hold the operator Node record without a key (stage 6), so the key of the VM that made them never works anywhere else
  opt no usable ready copies
    VM->>NT: Build the fleet<br/>then make ready copies
    %%> When:
    %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review: until the fleet build (`task_vm.fleet_build`) lands, the branch skips the fleet. The provision records "no nested fleet" (`nested_fleet: none`) on its result, and the VM stays usable for tasks that need no fleet. A proof run on such a VM fails early with `proof.no_nested_fleet`. When the fleet build lands, the branch calls it instead.
    %%> - a new task image has a seed with only the Node template until its first seed refresh (stage 6)
    %%> - copies of an older format count as not usable: the save writes `user.ready.format`, and the provision lists only the copies that carry the current value
    %%> Time:
    %%> - about 230 seconds; the build ends by making the ready copies for the next reset
  end
  SW->>NT: Apply the code<br/>migrate · restart
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the bind-mount units of the copies put `.env`, `storage` and `bootstrap/cache` (each guest's own disk) over the shared checkout at boot
  %%> - then the re-mint below, migrations and a restart of the long-running services (queue worker, scheduler, agent-view subscriber), so they run the checked-out code
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), container inner topology:
  %%> - the checkout is the disk device of the fleet start; there is no NFS export, NFS mount or mount ordering step any more
  %%> Why:
  %%> - an edit on the outer VM is in the nested Nodes at once, at local disk speed; nothing can run against old code
  SW->>NT: Re-mint the Swamp tokens<br/>nested Gateway · 30 days
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - runs the play `remint` of the Gateway bootstrap playbook on the nested Gateway, from the checkout disk device, with the control plan the build left on that machine (`/opt/orbit/bootstrap/control.yml`)
  %%> - the Swamp CLI renews the tokens by the names and into the files the build used: `access token rotate` for the serve driver, the two workers and the Gateway's run and read tokens, a new enrollment token for each worker; each lasts 30 days
  %%> - a worker gets a new enrollment token under a new name; the old one was used once and expires with the task VM, so nothing revokes it
  %%> - stops serve and both workers while the tokens change, then starts them; the apply step that follows restarts the Gateway's own services, which read the new tokens
  %%> - the worker units read the token files directly (group orbit, 0640), not through `LoadCredential=`, which does nothing in a container (chart `vm-fleet-build.mmd`)
  %%> - measured on shark: ok=15 failed=0, about 20 s
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the tokens of the ready copies are as old as the copies, so a fleet started from them would otherwise run a Swamp whose tokens expired; this step makes the age of the copies irrelevant, and the seed refresh needs no step for it
  %%> - the same fleet start does it every time, from the same checkout the apply uses; it needs no key from the real Gateway, because these tokens belong to the nested Orbit
  %%> - runs only when the VM has the proof Swamp Club key (a proof task): the Swamp CLI needs it, the start pushes it to `~/.config/swamp/api-key` of the nested Gateway before this step, and this step restarts serve and the workers so they read it
  %%> - the certificates last 397 days and are issued again by the seed refresh; nothing else in the fleet expires within the life of a task
  %%> Time:
  %%> - not yet measured; recorded here when the first provision with it has run
  opt playbooks or pins changed, also after a rebase
    SW->>VM: Converge once<br/>outer VM · nested Nodes
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - image playbooks on the outer VM; role playbooks and pins on the nested Nodes, through the nested Orbit's `node:converge`
    %%> - the nested Orbit reads the playbooks from the checkout disk device
    %%> - after a rebase onto main, a running task picks up the tools main added since its start
    %%> Why:
    %%> - a tool update lives in code; this puts it in place once, and the snapshot taken next keeps it
    %%> - the base images are not touched; only the merge and the seed refresh change them
  end
  VM->>NT: Check readiness<br/>agents · Swamp · Doctor
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - every Node agent online, the Swamp worker enrolled, the Gateway knows the operator, Doctor has no finding
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> - readiness first waits, bounded, until Doctor of the nested Gateway is healthy (one Ansible task with `until` and `retries`, the fleet build's wait playbook); the apply step restarts the agent-view subscriber, and the agents read online only after their next heartbeats, so a single read 3 s later failed (provision 11937)
  SW->>NT: Take the base snapshot<br/>containers · no memory
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one Incus snapshot `base` of each of the three containers, in parallel
  %%> - every proof batch starts from it (stage 3)
  %%> - holds the proof Swamp Club key, pushed in after the start from the ready copies, and the Swamp tokens renewed by this provision (30 days); accepted because `base` belongs to this task VM only and is destroyed with it; ready copies stay key-free
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), container inner topology:
  %%> - no memory state: a restore of `base` is a reboot of the containers, so the services start fresh, the clocks are the outer VM's, and nothing waits on an old network session
  %%> - measured: snapshot 0.3 s against 30–41 s for the stateful VM snapshot
  SW-->>GW: Environment ready<br/>terminal event
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the Gateway marks the group running and records the VM; the proof (stage 3) or the proof CI (stage 4) uses the fleet
  loop while the author develops
    VM->>NT: Edit, test, call the CLI<br/>the checkout is shared
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the author edits and runs tests on the outer VM; PHP in the nested containers sees the change on the next request
    %%> - the candidate CLI on the outer VM talks to the nested Gateway as the operator
    %%> - after a new migration or a change to a long-running service, the author runs the apply step again
    %%> - when the change holds playbooks or pins, or after a rebase onto main, the converge step above runs once
  end
  opt the author wants a clean fleet
    SW->>NT: Restore the base snapshot<br/>a reboot · 5–16 s
    %%> What it keeps:
    %%> - the outer VM, Pi and the checkout; the apply step follows, so the restored fleet runs the current code
  end
  SW->>H: Destroy the task VM<br/>always · success or failure
  %%> Why:
  %%> - no proof VM stays behind; diagnostics are kept, not running VMs
  %%> Target, not built:
  %%> - the Gateway then enrols a new slot to refill the pool of ready slots
